---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout>
    <section id="projects" class="py-24 max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-12 flex items-center gap-4">
            Soloing an Attack-Defense CTF - FAUST CTF 2026
        </h2>
        <p class="text-white text-sm mb-4">
        Going back to Thursday, the 24th of september 2026, I had my weekend practically free and I had wanted to play an Attack-Defense ever since late 2025, when I participated in Team Portugal for the ECSC 2025. I've been a member of STT since late 2024, but during 2025 and 2026 we only played jeopardy CTFs, never ADs (the short I will use for referring to 'Attack-Defense CTFs').

        <br><br>

        My colleagues had talked to me about an AD that was going to start that next Saturday - FAUST CTF 2026, and I saw an opportunity for a sprint challenge: design and setup the required infrastructure to play an AD.
        I knew I wouldn't be able to solo it, so I had two options:
        Focus on doing what I'm capable of as a single human being and accepting that I wouldn't be placed among the top 100, or use the help of AI.
        And if I were to use the help of LLMs, what would I use them for?

        <br><br>

        Being honest, I'm more of an apologist of the low-to-no LLM policies some recent CTFs have been implementing, considering nowadays' agentic models' capabilities.
        <br>
        I believe LLMs are great tools that should be used on real-life situations with precautions (verifying their output, actions, and always keeping the human accountable for the important decisions), but I believe the CTF culture has become centered on education, and LLM use threatens the culture by undermining the learning and problem-solving these events are meant to reward.
        <br>
        But I then remembered the lecture I attended earlier that week, on Tuesday, with professor David Obershaw from Stanford University, where he mentioned that most successful start-ups and businesses embraced new technologies by experimenting and adapting to them. I remember him vividly encouraging us to experiment with new technologies whenever we had the chance.
        <br>
        So that's what was on my mind when I decided to experiment with AI capabilities during that AD.
        <br>
        The next question was what would I use them for and at what level, which I decided to keep things simple: just use OpenCode on the source code and ask it to write exploits.
        <br>
        I also decided to use only Muse Spark 1.3, a free model by Meta.

        <br><br>

        With that being said, on Friday afternoon, I hopped on my PC and started to build infrastructure: One machine for the vulnbox (services that will be provided to me so I can get attacked by others) and another for the attacking tools and dashboards.
        <br>
        After around 6-7 hours of fixing breaking changes in an old Ansible configuration, adding my personal toolset, and purchasing and setting up virtual machines, I had everything set up and ready for the CTF.
        The only exception was the visual dashboards: I had barely tested them, since I was the only player and blue teamer, so I relied on alerts sent via webhooks.
        <br>
        I also wrote prompts to guide the AIs on what to do, how to test, and how to build the exploit.

        <br><br>

        On Saturday, one hour before the competition started, I tested their game network VPN and made sure everything was working while I decrypted and deployed the services.
        <br>
        There were four services: Alf, IMC (Interstellar Mission Control), Lamp and Rufflecopter.
        <br>
        I quickly identified Alf as the only one really related to Web and quickly opened 3 terminals with the AI for it to investigate the source code of the other challenges I wasn't going to focus on, looking for vulnerabilities and building their exploits.
        <br>
        I couldn't test Ataka (the tool I used for attacking), so I was 100% trusting the default templates, which turned out not to work and needed a quick fix that I only discovered beforehand.
        <br>
        Around 15 minutes into the pre-game hour I started digging into Alf and managed to find interesting behaviour in Alf. I tested many of my intuitions and, after about 5 failed attempts, at 47-48 minutes into the pre-game hour I proved the first (and simple) vulnerability.
        <br>
        I rushed to write the exploit using the template I mentioned previously, but I didn't think to account for the flag IDs, which indicate where each tick's flags are stored by the organization.
        <br>
        At this time, I was stressing because my proof of concept was written in spaghetti code and probably wasn't reproducible considering the flag id.

        <br><br>

        I then used the time until the first preliminary ticks (which are used to assess flag ids) to review the terminals running the AI models on each challenge to see whether they already had exploits or how they had progressed.
        <br>
        The results were impressive for the IMC service! The AI had built 2 exploits, and they were ready to deploy, according to it.
        <br>
        I couldn't say the same for the other services, though. The AI was still struggling to find any vulnerabilities at all.
        
        <br><br>

        In construction...
        

        </p>
        
    </section>
</Layout>
